---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import headerBg from '../../assets/photos/photo-13.jpg';

const DIR = '/blog/a-small-act-of-kindness';
---

<Base
  title="An Email and a Small Act of Kindness"
  description="A long email from a graduate student in Tehran, a patient reply from Heidi Nepf at MIT, and meeting her in person 17 years later. By Dr. Mohsen Tahmasebi Nasab."
  image={`${DIR}/share.jpg`}
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="// blog"
    title="An Email and a Small Act of Kindness"
    lede="A small act of kindness that stayed with me."
    bgImage={headerBg}
    bgPos="center 50%"
  >
    <p class="post-meta">Nov 8, 2026 · about a 4 minute read</p>
  </PageHeader>

  <article class="section">
    <div class="wrap post">
      <p class="kind-lead">
        We spend a lot of time talking about the technical side of engineering and science, and
        rightfully so. But I sometimes think we underestimate the human side of what we do. The way we
        interact with students, young engineers, and early-career scientists can shape their lives and
        careers in ways that neither they nor we may ever realize. I want to share a personal story
        about this.
      </p>
      <p>
        About 17 years ago, when I was starting my master's degree at the University of Tehran, I spent
        a lot of time reading journal papers, trying to figure out what interested me and what direction
        I wanted to take in research. One day, I came across a fascinating paper from Dr. Heidi Nepf's
        lab at MIT about the hydraulics of submerged vegetated channels. I was amazed by the physics of
        it all. And to be completely honest, most of the concepts and equations went right over my head!
        :) But I was curious. Really curious. So I decided to email Heidi.
      </p>
      <p>
        I wrote a long email, probably in pretty broken English, filled with questions, requests, and
        things I didn't understand. Looking back, I'm sure some of my questions were very basic.
        A few days later, I received a reply. Heidi had taken the time to
        respond to my questions, patiently share additional resources, and encourage me to keep reading
        and exploring.
      </p>
      <p>
        I still remember how that email made me feel. Here I was, a young graduate student in Tehran,
        thousands of miles away from MIT, and a world-renowned researcher had taken the time to help me
        simply because I was curious and wanted to learn. It meant a lot to me.
      </p>
      <p>
        Years later, I became a faculty member myself. And suddenly, that email meant even more. I began
        to understand how busy professors can be, how many emails and competing priorities fill their
        days, and how easy it would have been for Heidi to simply move on to the next email.
        But she didn't. And without knowing it, she had taught me something
        beyond hydraulics.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="kind-quote">
        She had shown me the kind of professor, mentor, and person I wanted to be.
      </blockquote>

      <figure class="post-fig kind-photo">
        <div
          class="kind-photo-img"
          role="img"
          aria-label="Heidi Nepf and Mohsen Tahmasebi Nasab standing together and smiling in front of a wood-paneled wall"
          style={`background-image:url(${DIR}/photo.jpg)`}
        ></div>
        <figcaption>With Heidi in Minneapolis, November 2026.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Fast forward about 17 years. I learned that Heidi was coming to Minneapolis to give a presentation.
        There was no way I was going to miss it! I attended her presentation and finally had the
        opportunity to meet her in person. I told her about that email from 17 years ago. I told her how
        much her kindness had meant to a young student in Tehran.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also shared a funny coincidence: Years after our email exchange, I became a visiting assistant
        professor at Bucknell University, the same university where she had earned her undergraduate
        degree back in the 1980s. What a small world!
      </p>
      <p>
        But most importantly, I got to tell her something she probably never knew: her
        kindness had shaped a part of my life, my career, and the way I try to treat others.
        And we took a photo together. That moment has earned a special place among my favorite memories.
      </p>
      <p>
        I keep thinking about how easy it would have been for Heidi to ignore that long email from a
        student with limited knowledge of hydrodynamics and imperfect English. She probably doesn't
        remember writing that email. For her, it might have been just another ordinary day. But for me,
        it was anything but ordinary.
      </p>

      <div class="kind-box">
        <p>
          And that's really the point of sharing this story. We don't always know the impact we
          have on others. Sometimes it's a thoughtful response to an email. Sometimes it's a few
          words of encouragement, a little patience, or simply taking someone seriously when they're
          still trying to find their way. Those moments may seem small to us, but they can stay with
          someone for a lifetime.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>
        And I believe kindness is something worth standing up for, even when it isn't the easiest or
        most convenient choice. I remember a time in my academic career when I strongly advocated for
        university leadership to send simple, personal messages to students who had been affected by a
        crisis. I was surprised by how much effort it took to make the case for something that seemed so
        basic to me. At one point, I received a response from leadership that felt completely
        disconnected from the human side of what those students were experiencing.
      </p>
      <p>
        I remember reminding them that their legacy wouldn't be defined by bigger buildings, fancier
        facilities, or higher rankings. Their real legacy would be the lives they touched and how they made people feel.

        I still believe that. And perhaps that's one of the most valuable lessons I learned from Heidi's
        email all those years ago.
      </p>
      <p>
        Kindness isn't always a grand gesture. Sometimes it's simply choosing to see the person on the
        other side.
      </p>

      <div class="kind-thanks">
        <p>
          Thank you, Heidi, and thank you to everyone who chooses to be kind, patient, and encouraging,
          especially when they don't have to. You may be making a bigger difference than you
          realize.
        </p>
        <p class="post-signoff">Mohsen :)</p>
      </div>

      <p class="post-back"><a href="/blog">← Back to blog</a></p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<script>
  // The photo is shown small and is not meant to be opened or saved: block the right-click menu and dragging on it.
  document.addEventListener('contextmenu', (e) => {
    if ((e.target as Element | null)?.closest?.('.kind-photo')) e.preventDefault();
  });
  document.addEventListener('dragstart', (e) => {
    if ((e.target as Element | null)?.closest?.('.kind-photo')) e.preventDefault();
  });
</script>

<style>
  .post-meta {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font: 600 0.82rem/1 var(--font-sans);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-faint);
  }

  .post > p {
    color: var(--text-dim);
    line-height: 1.75;
    margin: 0 0 1.15rem;
    font-size: 1.03rem;
  }

  .post-fig {
    margin: 0.35rem 0 1.2rem 2rem;
  }
  .kind-photo {
    float: right;
    width: 300px;
    user-select: none;
    -webkit-user-select: none;
    -webkit-touch-callout: none;
  }
  .kind-photo-img {
    aspect-ratio: 640 / 768;
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .post-fig figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.7rem;
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    color: var(--text-faint);
    line-height: 1.55;
    text-align: center;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .kind-photo { float: none; width: min(300px, 100%); margin: 1.6rem auto; }
  }

  .post > p.kind-lead {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .kind-quote {
    margin: 0.4rem 0 1.8rem;
    padding: 0.4rem 0 0.4rem 1.4rem;
    border-left: 4px solid var(--accent);
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--accent-2);
  }

  .kind-box {
    clear: both;
    margin: 1.8rem 0;
    padding: 1.4rem 1.6rem;
    background: var(--accent-soft);
    border: 1px solid var(--accent-line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  }
  .kind-box p, .kind-thanks p { margin: 0; color: var(--text-dim); line-height: 1.75; font-size: 1.03rem; }

  .kind-thanks {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    padding: 1.4rem 1.6rem;
    background: var(--bg-elev);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  }
  .kind-thanks .post-signoff { margin-top: 1rem; }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .kind-quote { font-size: 1.2rem; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .kind-photo { float: none; width: min(300px, 100%); margin: 1.6rem auto; }
  }

  .post-signoff {
    clear: both;
    margin-top: 2rem;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }
  .post-back {
    margin-top: 2.4rem;
    padding-top: 1.4rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
    font: 600 0.85rem/1 var(--font-sans);
  }
  .post-back a { color: var(--text-faint); }
  .post-back a:hover { color: var(--accent); }
</style>
